# Step 3 - Set Credential Value
Now that we have the credential schemas, we can construct the actual request to set the overwrites. On every single n8n startup, this sends an http request to the `/creds-overwrite` endpoint we enabled in step 1.

These values are secrets, just like passwords, so we set the credential values in a custom auth credential.

1. Using the format of the code block below, add the fields you want to overwrite from the schema under the credential name. 
2. Put this value in the custom auth credential in the `Register Overwrites` node. Ensure it's not in plain text anywhere and only set in the credential.
3. Publish the workflow. On the next instance restart, the overwrites will be set. _(Do not unpublish the workflow, it needs to run on every startup)_

_note: you can have an LLM assist you with the formatting, but DO NOT paste your api keys into an AI chat_


```
{
  "body": {
    "googleOAuth2Api": {
      "clientId": "***",
      "clientSecret": "***"
    },
    "todoistOAuth2Api": {
      "serverUrl": "***",
      "clientId": "***",
      "clientSecret": "***",
      "sendAdditionalBodyProperties": "***"
    }
  }
}
```

These nodes are what does the actual work. Keep the workflow published so it can run on every start up
